<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/foundational/verification-before-publication">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Journalism's entire value in an infinite-content world is one verb: <strong>verified</strong>.
    Anyone can publish a claim; a newsroom's product is the chain of checks behind it — sourcing,
    corroboration, comment, correction. This lesson is the newsroom's method, and it doubles as
    a citizen's toolkit for reading anything.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five checks every claim must survive" widget="equationStep" config={verifyCfg} />

  <h2>The reader's side: the inverted pyramid and its friends</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The inverted pyramid:</strong> the most important facts first, details after, background last. Written for readers who stop anywhere — and a good test of whether a writer knows what actually matters.</li>
    <li><strong>News vs opinion vs analysis:</strong> news reports what happened (verifiable); opinion argues from values (labelled); analysis explains why (interpretation, disclosed). Confusing the three is how trust dies.</li>
    <li><strong>Attribution is honesty about knowledge.</strong> "According to a person briefed on the matter" tells you exactly how strong the ground is — and that the journalist is telling you the truth about their footing.</li>
    <li><strong>Anonymous sources are a bargain:</strong> granted when information is vital and unobtainable on the record, and the editor knows who the source is. A paper that grants anonymity freely is selling its credibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Being a professional reader</h2>
  <p>
    Reverse the method. Who is the source, and do they know this first-hand? What would the
    correction look like if this is wrong? What is <em>not</em> mentioned? The strongest defence
    against misinformation isn't scepticism of everything — it's knowing what verification looks
    like and checking whether it happened.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SPJ Code of Ethics; Kovach & Rosenstiel, The Elements of Journalism; BBC and AP verification guidelines.</p>
</TopicPage>
